From: Eduardo Fockink <54868856+EduardoFockink@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 31 Oct 2026 18:50:42 -0300
Subject: [PATCH 1/3] drm: add AQ_MGPU_CPU_COPY, a CPU copy path for
 secondary-GPU outputs

On a hybrid laptop where the NVIDIA proprietary driver renders and the
display is wired to the Intel iGPU (USB-C/DP alt mode on a Dell G7 7588:
GTX 1050 Ti + UHD 630), the regular multi-GPU blit cannot work. The
secondary has to import the primary's buffer, NVIDIA cannot allocate the
LINEAR buffers that would make that possible, and the Intel/Mesa side
aborts when it imports the BLOCK_LINEAR one, which breaks login.

With AQ_MGPU_CPU_COPY=1 the secondary never imports or renders from the
primary's buffers. Instead, for an output on the secondary GPU:

- GBM: multi-GPU buffers keep the primary's native layout (no forced
  LINEAR), since only the primary reads them back.
- DRM: commitState() keeps a 2-buffer swapchain of linear dumb buffers on
  the secondary. The primary reads only the damaged region (plus the
  damage of the frames in between, as the swapchain rotates) straight
  into the CPU-mapped dumb buffer, which the secondary only scans out.
  Async commits are sent down the synchronous path.
- Renderer: readRegionInto() reads a region of a buffer into CPU memory
  with a given stride, sharing the FBO setup with readBuffer().

Cost scales with damage: ~0.2 ms of CPU per changed text line; a 5120x2160
full-frame copy is ~4.3 ms of CPU. 8-bit formats only ([AX]RGB8888,
[AX]BGR8888). AQ_MGPU_CPU_COPY_FULL=1 ignores damage (debug).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
